1-Longhorn can be installed on Rancher as a Rancher application Or on a separate RKE2 cluster 

2- Longhorn should be installed on the same rke2 cluster that runs the application pods

3- Longhorn Provides distributed block storage for Kubernetes 
====>  Distributed block storage refers to a storage architecture in which data is stored
across multiple physical devices or nodes, and these devices work together to provide a unified and scalable
storage system. (you can have multiple nodes in longhorn and data is spread across those nodes)

4- Prepare Storage: Longhorn requires dedicated storage for its default volume data path, which is
/var/lib/longhorn. Ensure you have a dedicated disk ready and mount it with the appropriate filesystem,
typically ext4 or xfs, to store Longhorn's volume data.

5- Have one or more additional dedicated disks that need to have a supported file system on them.

6-  important feature available is the ability to allocate a minimum amount of CPU to the Instance
manager. This allocation ensures that the Instance manager, responsible for controller and replica instance
lifecycle management, has the necessary computational resources to operate efficiently. By setting a
minimum CPU allocation, you can guarantee that Longhorn's core components receive the required
processing power for optimal performance.

7- V2 can be particularly attractive for:
databases
high-IOPS applications
NVMe
low-latency workloads
large storage systems

V1 is for general use. 

8- Choose V1 if:
You want the simplest deployment.
Your applications have normal I/O requirements.
You're running VMs/general applications/databases without extreme storage performance requirements.
You want maximum maturity.
You want fewer infrastructure prerequisites.
You don't have NVMe-focused hardware.

Consider V2 if:
You're using modern NVMe storage.
You need very high IOPS.
Latency matters significantly.
You have database workloads that can benefit from very fast storage.
You're comfortable managing SPDK/hugepages/hardware prerequisites.
You've tested the exact V2 feature set you need.

9- The Engine Image is the software that actually runs the Longhorn storage engine for a volume.
Think of it as the "storage engine binary/container". Longhorn needs an engine image installed/available on the nodes that will run the engine.

                  Longhorn Volume
                        │
                        ▼
                 Engine Image
              ┌─────────────────┐
              │ Longhorn Engine  │
              │                  │
              │ manages I/O      │
              │ replication      │
              │ snapshots        │
              └────────┬────────┘
                       │
             ┌─────────┼─────────┐
             ▼         ▼         ▼
          Replica 1  Replica 2  Replica 3

10- A backing image is essentially a read-only base image from which one or more Longhorn volumes can be created.
                Backing Image
                ┌────────────┐
                │ Ubuntu     │
                │ image      │
                │ 10 GB      │
                └─────┬──────┘
                      │
             ┌────────┼────────┐
             ▼        ▼        ▼
          Volume A  Volume B  Volume C
11- You normally don't create an Engine Image yourself. Longhorn creates/manages it.
You create a Backing Image only when you have a specific use case for starting volumes from pre-existing disk content.

12- Image Engine indicates: Which version of the storage engine (V1 or V2) should this volume use.

13- You create a backing image when you have some data that you want multiple Longhorn volumes to start from.

14- System backup = backup of longhorn itself

15- Data is replicated across the different nodes of the Longhorn cluster = HA

16- Rancher K8s Upgarde refers to upgrading the Kubernetes version of your downstream cluster managed by Rancher

17- trim feature is to reclaim storage from volume. = also with recurring job you can automate the trim operation. you will need to reclaim free storage 
when you delete some files or data and the volume size doesn't get reduced. 

18- for volume resize feature you need to be using the longhorn storage class and allowVolumeExpansion set to true. size can be increased but never decreased. 

19- data locality = Longhorn leverages the concept of scheduling pods on the same node where their associated Longhorn volumes are located.

20- when using strict-local data locality mode in Longhorn, the number of replicas for a volume should be set to 1.
strict-local setting enforces that Longhorn keeps exactly one single replica of the volume on the identical node 
where the workload (pod) is actively running.

21- to update the data locality for an existing volume you should know on which node your pod is running, go to the volume and chnage
the data locality and number of replica attach the volume to that node.

22- With best-effort data locality, every time your pod issues a write operation, that write goes to all replicas at the exact 
same millisecond, including the local one.

23- With strict-local the pod will issue write/read operation to only 1 volume on the same node of the pod. 

24- Data Locality Settings ExplainedIn distributed storage systems for Kubernetes (like Longhorn), 
Data Locality determines how storage replicas are placed relative to the Pod using them.
Strict-Local: Guarantees that one storage replica is always kept on the exact same node as the running Pod. 
It ensures 100% local reads and writes, minimizing network latency.

Best-Effort: The system tries to keep a replica on the Pod's node.
If that node lacks space or fails, the Pod still runs, and all storage traffic goes over the network to other nodes.

Disabled: Replica placement completely ignores where the Pod is running. 
Replicas are distributed across the cluster based purely on disk space and scheduling tags.

Scenario 1: Strict-Local and Pod ReschedulingIf you use Strict-Local and your Pod gets rescheduled to a different node:
The volume will attach to the new node.A new replica is immediately created on this new node.
Data begins syncing over the network from the old replica to the new local replica.
The old replica is deleted once the new local replica becomes fully healthy, maintaining your set replica count.

The Catch: While the new replica is syncing, your Pod will experience network latency because it has to read and write data
across the network from the old node.

Scenario 2: 3 Nodes, 2 Replicas, Best-Effort vs. DisabledYou have 3 nodes (Node A, Node B, Node C) and a volume with 2 replicas. 
Your Pod schedules onto Node A.
With Best-Effort:Placement: The system forces 1 replica onto Node A (the Pod's node).
The 2nd replica is placed on either Node B or Node C.
Performance: Excellent. Your Pod reads and writes directly to the local replica on Node A without network lag.

If Node A fails: The Pod can reschedule to Node B. 
Since it is "Best-Effort," the Pod will successfully start on Node B and talk to the remaining replica over the network while it tries to build a new local replica.

With Disabled:
Placement: The 2 replicas are placed randomly across the 3 nodes (e.g., Node B and Node C). 
Node A might get no replica at all.
Performance: If Node A has no replica, every single read and write operation from your Pod must travel over the cluster network to Node B or C.
If Node A fails: The Pod moves to another node, but its storage performance remains dictated by random network placement.

25- you can specify the data locality in a strogeclass that ou create manually and use it for PVC/PV creation.

26- Replica Auto-Balance in Longhorn helps maintain data redundancy and load distribution in the cluster by
automatically redistributing volume replicas as needed when a new node added in the cluster. This ensures
that the storage infrastructure remains efficient, resilient, and well-balanced, making it a valuable feature for
managing data in a distributed storage environment.
Longhorn offers three choices for the global replica auto-balance setting:

Disabled (Default): In this mode, no automatic replica auto-balancing occurs.

Least-Effort: This option prompts Longhorn to balance replicas with minimal redundancy adjustments. For
instance, on your node-1 there are 4 off-balanced replicas when a new node node-2 is added, a volume with
four off-balanced replicas will only rebalance one replica.

Best-Effort: Selecting this mode instructs Longhorn to make the best effort to balance replicas evenly for
optimal redundancy. For example, on your node-1 there are 4 off-balanced replicas after adding a new node
node-2, a volume with four off-balanced replicas will have two replicas rebalanced.
